// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// logic — propositional primitives over `Prop` (D63 §8.3 Phase 0).
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The `Prop` combinators the DCG determiner/connective semantics need but the
// kernel doesn't provide built-in. A small, reusable layer: depends only on
// core (`Prop` = `Sort 0`). The semantics built on it stay kernel-checked — each
// definition here is a closed `Prop`(-former) the validator admits on commit.
//
// `False` (the determiner `no` needs negation, `¬P := P -> logic:False`),
// `And`, and `Or` — the last two consumed by the generalized-conjunction
// combinator (D63 §8.4 Phase 3) and eliminated by the FraCaS conjunction
// inference (Phase 5).

namespace logic = "urn:eigenius:logic";

// Bottom — the empty proposition (zero constructors: no proof can be built).
// Negation is the idiom `P -> logic:False`; e.g. the determiner `no` denotes
// `λA. λV. ∀x:A. (V(x) -> logic:False)`. (Mirrors the `stats:False` idiom; this
// is its namespace-neutral home — statistics may migrate to it later.)
data logic:False : Prop { }

// Conjunction — the product of two propositions. The DCG generalized-conjunction
// combinator (D63 §8.4 Phase 3) builds `logic:And(P, Q)` as the meaning of a
// coordinated `S` (and pointwise-lifts it for `VP`/`TV` coordination).
//
// `P`, `Q` are PARAMETERS (sort-typed at `Prop`), mirroring Lean's
// `And (a b : Prop) : Prop`. Because parameters are fixed across the recursor's
// motive (only indices are abstracted), first-projection (`P ∧ Q ⊨ P`) is the
// ordinary parametric recursor — which is what makes the FraCaS conjunction
// inference (D63 §8.4 Phase 5) a plain elimination rather than needing the
// harder index-abstracting recursor.
// The constructor type leads with the parameter binders (`forall (P, Q)`), the
// kernel convention for parametrized inductives — `peel_ctor_telescope` strips
// `params.len()` leading binders to expose the constructor's own fields (`P`-proof,
// `Q`-proof). What makes `P`,`Q` parameters rather than indices is the `(P, Q)` in
// the *data header*, not the constructor shape.
data logic:And (P : Prop, Q : Prop) : Prop {
    conj : forall (P : Prop, Q : Prop) => P -> Q -> logic:And(P, Q)
}

// Disjunction — the sum of two propositions (`or`). Parameters, as for `And`.
data logic:Or (P : Prop, Q : Prop) : Prop {
    inl : forall (P : Prop, Q : Prop) => P -> logic:Or(P, Q),
    inr : forall (P : Prop, Q : Prop) => Q -> logic:Or(P, Q)
}
